  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1052 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nGlobal model guidance suggests that light south-southeasterly \nflow will persist across the TN Valley for much of the extended \nperiod, perhaps becoming a bit more pronounced on Sunday and \nMonday as a low develops across the central High Plains and \nprogresses northeastward into the Great Lakes. This will sustain a\nvery moist airmass (featuring PWAT values in the 2-2.2\" range and\ndewpoints in the u60s-l70s, perhaps rising into the m70s by \nMonday and Tuesday). Although showers and thunderstorms could \noccur at any point (day or night), storm coverage during any \nsingle specific period remains uncertain due to indications that \nthe Atlantic subtropical ridge will retrograde westward into the \nsoutheastern CONUS with time. Weak shear suggests that storms will\nbe unorganized and initiated by low-level warm advection and \nmesoscale boundary interactions. Regardless, sufficiently high \nCAPE will exist for frequent lightning and high PWAT values/slow \ncell motions will support a risk for excessive rainfall and flash \nflooding. Highs are generally predicted to be in the l-m 90s (but \nmay be lower on days with more extensive clouds and \nprecipitation), with lows in the u60s-l70s. Heat risk will remain \nin the Minor-Moderate category through the period.",
